  nonce: a6e8  # a6e8
  data:  # a6e8
    kind: work_item  # a6e8
    title: "java has 69 taint sinks and a CFG mapping but NO def/use extractor exists, so atomic_statement alone cannot help it"  # a6e8
    status: todo_hard  # a6e8
    priority: 2  # a6e8
    parent:  # a6e8
    tags:  # a6e8
    - adr_0017  # a6e8
    - taint  # a6e8
    - analysis_quality  # a6e8
    isbefore: []  # a6e8
    duplicate_of: []  # a6e8
    not_duplicate_of: []  # a6e8
    pr_ref:  # a6e8
    description: "FOUND WHILE WIRING WI-tohuk, 2026-08-03.\n\nWI-tohuk names rust, typescript AND java as lacking atomic_statement. True for\nall three, but the remedy differs and the item does not say so: rust and\ntypescript already had extractors and needed only wiring, whereas java has NO\ndef/use extractor at all. Only four exist: py_def_use, go_def_use,\nrust_def_use, ts_def_use. Adding atomic_statement to java.yaml would therefore\nleave java emitting zero DDG edges -- the YAML key is necessary and nowhere\nnear sufficient.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH DOING ANYWAY: java carries 45 sources and 69 SINKS, and 67 of\nthose 69 sinks are method-kind (97%, the highest ratio of any language). It\nalready has cfg_nodes/java.yaml with conditional / loop / try_catch / switch\nhooks, so the CFG half is done.\n\nSCOPE: write JavaDefUseExtractor (local_variable_declaration, assignment via\nexpression_statement, enhanced-for bindings, catch parameters), register it,\nadd atomic_statement to java.yaml, register a LanguageDdgSpec. The new\ntest_ddg_language_wiring gate will then cover it automatically once the\nextractor is registered -- add java to EXPECTED_DEF_USE_LANGUAGES in the\nsame PR so the gate does not silently keep quantifying over three languages.\n\nSEQUENCING NOTE: worth less than the javascript item (83 sinks, no extractor\nneeded) despite being closer to done."  # a6e8
    fields: {}  # a6e8
- op: discuss  # fd5d
  at: '2026-08-25T21:40:11Z'  # fd5d
  by: agent  # fd5d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # fd5d
  clock: 2  # fd5d
  nonce: fd5d  # fd5d
  message: "MEASURED 2026-08-25, four purpose-built fixtures, java arm and python control\nrun SEPARATELY so each verdict and exit code is attributable. Artifacts +\nraw --json: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/p4_gotun_08252026/.\n\nPREMISE VERIFIED IN THE CODE, and it still holds exactly as filed: java has a\ncfg_nodes mapping and no def/use extractor, no LanguageDdgSpec, and no\natomic_statement. Sink count is unchanged at 69; sources have grown 45 -> 48.\n\nTHE \"OR DECLARE BLIND\" HALF IS ALREADY SHIPPED, which this item predates and\ntherefore does not know. verify-claims --json carries a per-language\ndataflow_coverage.languages row that names this item's gap in machine-readable\nform, one bit per blocker rather than a single \"supported?\" boolean:\n\n  {\"language\": \"java\", \"dataflow_capable\": false,\n   \"blockers\": [\"atomic_statement\", \"def_use_extractor\", \"ddg_spec\"],\n   \"cfg_mapping\": true, \"catalog_sinks\": 69, \"catalog_sources\": 48}\n\nSo the silence this item guards against is not silent. There is nothing left\nto declare, and no dated blindness declaration needs writing.\n\nWHAT THE MISSING EXTRACTOR ACTUALLY COSTS, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. It\ndoes NOT cost recall on the shapes tested:\n\n  (1) SAME-FUNCTION flow, env_read -> local -> local -> fs_write, java and\n      python arms in one repo. BOTH arms report it. java: one finding,\n      analysis_method=structural (System.getenv -> FileWriter.write). python:\n      two, ddg and ddg_mixed. The count differs only because python's\n      catalogue carries builtins.open as a second matching row; the FLOW is\n      found by both. Verdict violated, rc 1.\n  (2) CROSS-FILE flow, source in one file, sink in another. BOTH arms MISS it\n      and return rc 0 confirmed with findings_total 0 -- python included, at\n      dataflow_capable=true. This is the already-published section 3a\n      intraprocedural limit (WI-tizig, docs/VERIFY-CLAIMS-SCOPE.md), not a\n      java gap. Running the control is what established that.\n  (3) CO-LOCATED NON-FLOW negative control, source and sink in one file in\n      unrelated functions. java rc 3 confirmed_with_caveats\n      (unknown_receiver_scope); python rc 0 bare confirmed. java is MORE\n      conservative here, not less -- it did not over-report the co-location.\n\nWHY RECALL IS UNAFFECTED, from section 3a's own contract rather than from the\nfixtures alone: the walk is CONFIRM-ONLY and dataflow_scope publishes\ninclusion_decided_by=\"call_graph_reachability\" as a constant. Every reported\nflow, INCLUDING every ddg one, was included by call-graph reachability; the\nDDG raises confidence on a flow already in the set and never adds or removes\none. A language without a DDG therefore reports the same flow population and\nlabels all of it structural.\n\nTHE CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ITEM'S PRIORITY, which is the part that changes. The\nextractor buys exactly two things: (a) java flows become labelable ddg instead\nof uniformly structural/approximate, so a java reader can tell a confirmed data\ndependence from a co-location; and (b) java becomes eligible for section 3a\nREFUTATION -- but 3a cannot refute for ANY language today, so (b) is gated\nbehind WI-kabif, which is itself todo_hard and parked on a number. Until\nWI-kabif lands, building this extractor changes confidence LABELS on java\nfindings and nothing else.\n\nSCOPE OF THIS MEASUREMENT, stated rather than implied: three flow shapes, two\nlanguages, synthetic fixtures. It does not measure how many java findings in a\nreal repo would relabel structural -> ddg, and it does not establish that no\nfourth shape discriminates. It does establish that the two obvious candidates\n(same-function and cross-file) do not.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: leave todo_hard, do NOT build now. The blindness half is\nclosed by dataflow_coverage; the extractor half is precision-labeling work\nwhose refutation payoff is gated behind WI-kabif, and the standing band ruling\nhas recall work stopped pending the owner's re-rank. Reconsider when WI-kabif\nlands, at which point (b) becomes real and this item's value changes."  # fd5d
- op: discuss  # bfb2
  at: '2026-08-25T21:46:39Z'  # bfb2
  by: agent  # bfb2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bfb2
  clock: 3  # bfb2
  nonce: bfb2  # bfb2
  message: "AMENDMENT TO THE PRECEDING ENTRY, same day, found by verifying its own\nload-bearing claim in the code instead of resting on the module docstring. The\nrecall conclusion holds and is now confirmed at the statement level; the COST\nmodel in that entry was incomplete in a way that matters for ranking.\n\nRECALL CONCLUSION CONFIRMED IN CODE (taint.py ~3345, ~3440). The finding is\nappended in ALL THREE branches; the walk only chooses the label:\n\n    adjudicated = _ddg_taint_reaches(...) is True   # CONFIRM-ONLY\n    if adjudicated:  confidence=\"precise\";     method=\"ddg\"\n    elif fn_has_ddg: confidence=\"approximate\"; method=\"ddg_mixed\"\n    else:            confidence=\"approximate\"; method=\"structural\"\n\nwith the in-code comment \"raises confidence ... and never removes a flow\". A\nlanguage with no DDG reports the same flow population, uniformly structural.\n\nWHAT THE PRECEDING ENTRY MISSED: THERE IS AN UNGATED PRECISION COST, and it\nruns in the FALSE-POSITIVE direction. The WI-fasub same-function sanitizer arm\n(taint.py ~3397) is guarded by\n\n    if adjudicated and barrier_sites:\n\nso crediting a sanitizer called in the SAME function as the source requires a\nDDG confirmation. java can never be `adjudicated`, so java can NEVER credit a\nsame-function sanitizer. The canonical shape -- encrypt-then-write in one\nfunction, INV-pojib's os.remove(launder(os.environ[\"API_KEY\"])) -- reports a\nviolation in java that python correctly suppresses. That is a FALSE POSITIVE\njava carries and python does not, and unlike the section 3a refutation payoff\nit is NOT gated behind WI-kabif: it would be recovered the day the extractor\nlands.\n\nSO THE WORK IS PRECISION WORK, NOT RECALL WORK, which puts it on the\nFAVOURED side of the band ruling rather than the stopped side. That is the\nopposite of the preceding entry's implication and is the reason this amendment\nexists rather than a footnote.\n\nBUT THE CEILING IS SMALL, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, and this is what keeps\nthe recommendation unchanged. From the same fixture run's dataflow_coverage:\n\n    java  : catalog_sanitizers = 1\n    python: catalog_sanitizers = 4\n    sanitizer_scope.sanitizable_labels = [\"plaintext\"]\n    sanitizer_scope.taint_categories   = [\"plaintext -> ciphertext\"]\n\njava's sanitizer catalogue holds exactly ONE entry, so the whole\nsame-function-sanitizer benefit is bounded by how often that single sanitizer\nappears in the same function as a source. Not zero, and not measured on a real\njava repo -- but it cannot be large while the catalogue has one row.\n\nRECOMMENDATION UNCHANGED, REASONING SHARPENED. Still do not build now, but the\nreason is no longer \"the payoff is gated\" -- part of it is ungated. The reason\nis that the ungated part is bounded by a ONE-ROW sanitizer catalogue, and the\nunbounded part is still gated behind WI-kabif. A cheaper item with a strictly\nlarger effect on the same axis: ADD SANITIZER ROWS TO java.yaml, which needs\nno extractor and raises the ceiling this item would then be able to collect.\nIf that is done first, re-rank this item, because its payoff scales with that\ncatalogue.\n\nSTILL NOT MEASURED, stated so the gap is visible: how many same-function\nsanitizer sites a real java repo actually contains. The corpus here is\npython + go, so this cannot be answered from it."  # bfb2
